In TypeScript, you can use the "as" operator to tell the type system that you know a value will only have a specific type, even though it may have multiple types in theory. For example:

let myValue: string | number = "hello";
myValue = 42;

// This is valid TypeScript code, but the type system doesn't know that myValue will only have a number type in practice
console.log(myValue.length); // Error: Property 'length' does not exist on type 'number'

let myValue2: string | number = "hello";
myValue2 = 42;
myValue2 = myValue2 as number;

// This is also valid TypeScript code, but now we're explicitly telling the type system that we know myValue2 will only have a number type in practice
console.log(myValue2.length); // No error, because we've told the type system that myValue2 is a number
```

In this example, we have a variable `myValue` that can have either a string or a number type. We then assign a number to it, but the type system doesn't know that it will only have a number type in practice. This can cause errors when we try to access properties that only exist on one of the possible types.

To solve this, we can use the "as" operator to explicitly tell the type system that we know `myValue2` will only have a number type in practice. This allows us to access properties that only exist on the number type without causing errors.

